1. Workload: 100000 objects of 512 bytes each written, modified once, and deleted within 30 seconds. Compaction trigger runs every 60 seconds. Without proposal, each object requires two writes (original and modified unit), totaling 65536 bytes per object, 6553600000 bytes total. With proposal, each object incurs initial write (32768), compaction container write (32768), mapping update (16384), modification write (32768), and mapping update (16384), totaling 131072 bytes per object, 13107200000 bytes total. Proposal writes 13.1 GB vs 6.55 GB without, never pays off.

2. Workload: One object modified five times consecutively, with compaction triggered after each modification. With proposal: initial write (32768) + five cycles of modification write (32768), container write (32768), and mapping update (16384) per cycle. Total: 32768 + 5*(32768 + 32768 + 16384) = 442368 bytes. Without proposal: six writes (initial + five modifications), 6*32768 = 196608 bytes. Proposal writes 442368 bytes vs 196608 bytes without. Trigger policy: skip compaction for objects modified within last hour. Cost: 8 bytes per object for timestamp storage and additional processing time to check timestamps during compaction.

3. Workload: Create 58 objects of 512 bytes, pack into one container, then delete all objects. After deletion, container remains with 100% dead slots. Standing occupancy: 32768 bytes. Without proposal, deletion frees all units, standing occupancy 0 bytes. Proposal loses when container has 100% dead slots.

4. Scenario: A single container holding 58 objects of 512 bytes becomes unreadable due to disk corruption. All 58 objects lost. Without proposal, a single unit corruption loses one object. To make acceptable: implement container replication (e.g., RAID2 copies stored on separate disks) or RAIDerasure coding to recover from single unit failure.

5. Tried variable-sized containers but unit size is fixed at 32768 bytes. Tried mutable containers but modifications require rewriting entire container, defeating immutability. Tried eliminating central mapping but object location tracking became infeasible. Tried immediate container freeing for all-dead containers but scanning all containers for dead slots is prohibitively expensive.
